Chapter: StrConcatInPlace

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Is `__str_concat`'s in-place path EVER taken?

 `emit-str-concat-prologue` (`X86_64TextHelpers.codex:160-182`) compares
 `left_base + aligned(len(left))` against the allocation frontier and, when
 they meet, `emit-str-concat-fast-copy` rewrites the length in place instead
 of copying. `literal-alloc` showed a Text literal allocates nothing and that
 hoisting it out of the loop changes nothing, so in `acc & "x"` the
 accumulator IS the most recent allocation from the second iteration on and
 the fast path should fire. It measured quadratic anyway.

 So this asks the question without a loop, where nothing can be blamed on
 recursion or tail calls: append the same piece one, two and three times in a
 straight line and read the DELTAS. In-place extension costs one piece per
 append. Copying costs the whole accumulator each time, so the deltas grow.

Section: The Instrument

  keep-text : Text -> Integer
  keep-text (t) = text-length t

  mk : Integer, Text -> Text
  mk (n) (acc) = if n <= 0 then acc else mk (n - 1) (acc & "abcdefgh")

Section: Arms

 `piece` is built BEFORE any measurement so it is a heap value and not a
 literal, and every arm below starts from `base-acc`, itself already on the
 heap, so no arm's first append is the constant-left-operand case.

  piece : Text = mk 8 ""

  base-acc : Text = mk 8 ""

  one : Text -> Text
  one (p) = base-acc & p

  two : Text -> Text
  two (p) = (base-acc & p) & p

  three : Text -> Text
  three (p) = ((base-acc & p) & p) & p

Section: Measurement

  m-one : Integer
  m-one =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (one piece)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-two : Integer
  m-two =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (two piece)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-three : Integer
  m-three =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep-text (three piece)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: Entry

 The reading. `piece` is 64 characters and `base-acc` is 64. If the fast path
 fires, two costs about one piece more than one, and three about one piece
 more than two, so the deltas are flat. If every append copies, one costs
 128, two costs 128 + 192, three costs 128 + 192 + 256, and the deltas climb
 by one piece each time.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("piece-len=" & show (text-length piece) & " base-len=" & show (text-length base-acc))
    print-line-uni ("one   = " & show m-one)
    print-line-uni ("two   = " & show m-two)
    print-line-uni ("three = " & show m-three)
    print-line-uni ("delta two-one   = " & show (m-two - m-one))
    print-line-uni ("delta three-two = " & show (m-three - m-two))
  end
